lligent Information\n                                             Processing, Shenzhen Key Laboratory                             Processing, Shenzhen Key Laboratory                 Processing, Shenzhen Key Laboratory\n                                                      of Media Security,                                               of Media Security,                                 of Media Security,\n                                                     Shenzhen University                                             Shenzhen University                                 Shenzhen University\n                                                       Shenzhen, China                                                 Shenzhen, China                                     Shenzhen, China\n                                              xuyuxiong2022@email.szu.edu.cn                                   linkaiqing2021@email.szu.edu.cn                             libin@szu.edu.cn\n\n                                                                                               Haodong Li                                                Sheng Li\n                                                                                  Guangdong Provincial Key                               Afirstsoft Technology Group Co., Ltd.\n                                                                             Laboratory of Intelligent Information                                  Shenzhen, China\n                                                                             Processing, Shenzhen Key Laboratory                                 admin@tenorshare.cn\n                                                                                       of Media Security,\narXiv:2607.26553v1 [cs.SD] 29 Jul 2026\n\n\n\n\n                                                                                     Shenzhen University\n                                                                                       Shenzhen, China\n                                                                                    lihaodong@szu.edu.cn\n\n                                         Abstract                                                                                         CCS Concepts\n                                         Existing audio forgery detection and localization (AFDL) methods                                 • Security and privacy → Spoofing attacks; • Computing\n                                         often overfit dataset-specific low-level artifacts, limiting their gen-                          methodologies → Artificial intelligence.\n                                         eralization to subtle, localized, and unseen manipulations. Recent\n                                         audio large language model (ALLM)-based approaches cast AFDL                                     Keywords\n                                         as question answering but still model forensic evidence implicitly,                              Audio Forgery Detection and Localization, Chain-of-Thought, Modality-\n                                         without linking manipulation cues to predictions. To bridge this gap,                            Incremental Learning\n                                         we propose ThinkOmni, a reasoning-driven omni-modal large lan-\n                                         guage model that jointly performs explicit forensic reasoning, spoof-                            ACM Reference Format:\n                                         ing detection, and temporal manipulation localization. To enable                                 Yuxiong Xu, Kaiqing Lin, Bin Li, Haodong Li, and Sheng Li. 2026. ThinkOmni:\n                                         explicit reasoning supervision, we construct Forensic-Aware Chain-                               A Reasoning-Driven Omni-Modal LLM Framework for Audio Forgery De-\n                                         of-Thought (FACoT), a 100K-sample dataset with structured forensic                               tection and Localization. In Proceedings of the 34th ACM International Con-\n                                         evidence and reasoning annotations. Leveraging FACoT, we intro-                                  ference on Multimedia (MM ’26). ACM, New York, NY, USA, 21 pages.\n                                                                                                                                          https://doi.org/10.1145/3767308.3835017\n                                         duce Forensic-Aware Modality-Incremental Learning (FMIL), which\n                                         progressively aligns semantic, acoustic, and spectral-visual repre-\n                                         sentations with the LLM backbone to capture complementary foren-                                 1    Introduction\n                                         sic cues. We further propose Forensic-Consistent Multi-task Loss\n                                                                                                                                          Advances in generative models have expanded the scope of audio\n                                         (FCML), which combines weighted cross-entropy with an adaptive\n                                                                                                                                          spoofing from fully synthetic speech to increasingly fine-grained\n                                         localization loss to coordinate reasoning generation, spoofing detec-\n                                                                                                                                          partial manipulations [20, 22, 37, 40]. By altering only short tem-\n                                         tion, and temporal localization. Extensive experiments show that\n                                                                                                                                          poral segments while preserving most of the original recording,\n                                         ThinkOmni achieves strong cross-dataset generalization in both de-\n                                                                                                                                          partial deepfakes leave localized and less perceptible forensic traces,\n                                         tection and localization. Code, models, data, and inference examples\n                                                                                                                                          posing substantial challenges to forgery detection and temporal\n                                         are available at https://beyond0814.github.io/ThinkOmni/.\n                                                                                                                                          localization, especially under cross-dataset evaluation [48, 52].\n                                         ∗ Corresponding author.                                                                              Recent studies [5, 23, 55] address AFDL through two paradigms:\n                                                                                                                                          self-supervised learning (SSL)-based methods and audio large lan-\n                                         Permission to make digital or hard copies of all or part of this work for personal or            guage model (ALLM)-based methods. Although they differ in for-\n                                         classroom use is granted without fee provided that copies are not made or distributed            mulation, both rely largely on implicit representations learned from\n                                         for profit or commer